.md
## Frontend support for string operators
We extended the lightweight WL interpreter that runs in the frontend with more string-related operators, including `ToString`, `NumberForm`, `StringJoin`, and `Round`. This is useful for frontend-side formatting and label generation.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Frontend support for string operators
We extended the lightweight WL interpreter that runs in the frontend with more string-related operators, including `ToString`, `NumberForm`, `StringJoin`, and `Round`. This is useful for frontend-side formatting and label generation.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
sqval = 0.3;
EventHandler[InputRange[0.33,1,0.1], (sqval=(*SqB[*)Sqrt[#](*]SqB*))&]

TextView[
  sqval // Offload,
  "Label" -> "Before"
]

TextView[
  StringJoin["sqval = ", 
    ToString[NumberForm[sqval, {4,2}]
  ]] // Offload,
  "Label" -> "After"
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
These operators also work under `Offload`, because they are defined as frontend symbols.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
These operators also work under `Offload`, because they are defined as frontend symbols.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Here is another example that uses `Round`.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Here is another example that uses `Round`.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ln = Table[{i, 5.0 Sin[i/25.0 Pi]}, {i,0,50,0.04}];
Graphics[{
  Red, Line[ln//Offload],
  Blue, Line[ln//Round//Offload],
  EventHandler[AnimationFrameListener[ln//Offload], (
    ln = Transpose[{ln[[All,1]], RotateLeft[ln[[All,2]], 1]}]
  )&]
}, ImageSize->150]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## Improved support for packed arrays
We improved how packed arrays are stored and transferred from the Wolfram Kernel to the frontend. During initial evaluation, `Graphics` and `Image` expressions are usually serialized as `"ExpressionJSON"` and reinterpreted in the frontend; subsequent updates use binary WXF. That initial pass was inefficient because numeric and packed arrays were serialized as plain lists by default, increasing payload sizes by 10-100 times. We patched the standard JSON serializer so packed and numeric arrays are sent as base64-encoded WXF strings. This matters especially for data structures such as `Image` and `Graphics3D` objects with many vertices.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Improved support for packed arrays
We improved how packed arrays are stored and transferred from the Wolfram Kernel to the frontend. During initial evaluation, `Graphics` and `Image` expressions are usually serialized as `"ExpressionJSON"` and reinterpreted in the frontend; subsequent updates use binary WXF. That initial pass was inefficient because numeric and packed arrays were serialized as plain lists by default, increasing payload sizes by 10-100 times. We patched the standard JSON serializer so packed and numeric arrays are sent as base64-encoded WXF strings. This matters especially for data structures such as `Image` and `Graphics3D` objects with many vertices.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ComplexPlot3D[(*FB[*)(((*SpB[*)Power[Sin[z](*|*),(*|*)3](*]SpB*))(*,*)/(*,*)((*SpB[*)Power[(z+1)(*|*),(*|*)4](*]SpB*)))(*]FB*), {z, -5 - 5 I, 5 + 5 I}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["22af5701-3668-44e0-a384-d673c15f2f5d"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKGxklppmaGxjqGpuZWeiamKQa6CYaW5joppiZGycbmqYZpZmmAABzwhUT"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
